1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:15,443
In April 1977, in Revolution Square in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia's military leader stood before a huge crowd. He raised bottles filled with red liquid, and smashed them on the ground.

2
00:00:16,543 --> 00:00:26,335
The red stood for the blood of the revolution's enemies. Over the next two years, a campaign known as the Red Terror swept through Ethiopia's cities.

3
00:00:27,435 --> 00:00:37,461
Estimates of the dead range from tens of thousands to, according to Amnesty International, as many as half a million. And the famine was still to come.

4
00:00:38,561 --> 00:00:54,241
Mengistu Haile Mariam ruled Ethiopia for seventeen years. In 2006, an Ethiopian court convicted him of genocide. As of 2025, he was still living in exile. This is his story.

5
00:00:56,441 --> 00:01:10,201
For more than four decades, Ethiopia was ruled by Emperor Haile Selassie. Abroad, he was admired as a statesman. At home, most people were poor peasants working land owned by nobles and the church.

6
00:01:11,301 --> 00:01:21,563
In the early 1970s, a famine in the northern province of Wollo killed tens of thousands of people. The government tried to keep it hidden.

7
00:01:22,663 --> 00:01:37,276
A British television documentary exposed it in 1973. Later, on the eve of the emperor's fall, Ethiopian television broadcast that film, cut together with scenes of palace luxury.

8
00:01:38,376 --> 00:01:50,237
Mengistu was born around 1937, into a family of modest means. He joined the army as a teenager and trained at the Holeta military academy.

9
00:01:51,337 --> 00:02:04,265
He was ambitious, and resented the aristocrats who dominated the officer corps. He received some military training in the United States, and by 1974 he was a major.

10
00:02:06,465 --> 00:02:17,708
In 1974, discontent boiled over. Soldiers mutinied over pay and conditions, and students, teachers and taxi drivers went on strike.

11
00:02:18,808 --> 00:02:29,411
Junior officers formed a committee of about a hundred and twenty men, known as the Derg, which simply means committee. Mengistu became one of its leading figures.

12
00:02:30,511 --> 00:02:41,881
On the twelfth of September 1974, the Derg deposed Haile Selassie. The emperor was driven away from his palace in the back of a small Volkswagen.

13
00:02:42,981 --> 00:02:51,984
Two months later, the Derg executed sixty former officials in a single night, without trial, including two former prime ministers.

14
00:02:53,084 --> 00:03:05,649
The new regime declared itself socialist. In 1975 it nationalised all rural land, breaking the power of the old landlords. Many peasants welcomed it.

15
00:03:06,749 --> 00:03:15,432
It launched mass literacy campaigns that won international praise. For a while, many Ethiopians believed in the revolution.

16
00:03:16,532 --> 00:03:28,009
In August 1975, the old emperor died in custody. The regime said he had died of natural causes. It is widely believed that he was murdered.

17
00:03:30,209 --> 00:03:46,337
Inside the Derg, Mengistu removed his rivals one by one. In February 1977, after a shootout at a Derg meeting, the head of state, Teferi Bante, and several others were killed. Mengistu was now in sole charge.

18
00:03:47,437 --> 00:03:59,704
His fiercest opponents were Marxists too. A civilian group, the Ethiopian People's Revolutionary Party, the EPRP, had begun assassinating supporters of the regime.

19
00:04:00,804 --> 00:04:10,105
Mengistu answered with the Red Terror. Neighbourhood associations, called kebeles, were armed and given the power to arrest and to kill.

20
00:04:11,205 --> 00:04:26,373
Young people suspected of opposition were rounded up and shot, and their bodies left in the streets as a warning. According to survivors and human rights groups, some families were made to pay for the bullets before they could bury their children.

21
00:04:27,473 --> 00:04:39,377
Tens of thousands were imprisoned, and torture was widespread. Rival left-wing groups were destroyed, along with much of a generation of educated young Ethiopians.

22
00:04:41,577 --> 00:04:58,879
In 1977, Somalia invaded Ethiopia's Ogaden region. The Soviet Union switched sides to back Mengistu, sending weapons, advisers and thousands of Cuban troops. Somalia was driven back in 1978.

23
00:04:59,979 --> 00:05:09,963
In the north, wars against rebels in Eritrea and Tigray dragged on for years. Mengistu built one of the largest armies in Africa to fight them.

24
00:05:11,063 --> 00:05:20,791
He ruled through the Derg, and from 1984 through a new Workers' Party of Ethiopia, modelled on Soviet communism.

25
00:05:22,991 --> 00:05:32,569
In 1983, drought struck northern Ethiopia again. It hit hardest in Wollo and in the war zones of Tigray and Eritrea.

26
00:05:33,669 --> 00:05:47,259
In September 1984, as people starved in the north, the regime staged lavish celebrations for the tenth anniversary of the revolution. News of the famine was played down.

27
00:05:48,359 --> 00:05:56,508
Then, in October 1984, BBC television reports showed the world the camps of the starving.

28
00:05:57,608 --> 00:06:07,677
The response led to Band Aid, and in July 1985 to the Live Aid concerts, which raised huge sums for famine relief.

29
00:06:08,777 --> 00:06:13,855
Estimates of the dead range from about four hundred thousand to one million.

30
00:06:14,955 --> 00:06:25,621
The regime used the crisis to move hundreds of thousands of people from the north to the south, often by force. Many died on the way or in the new settlements.

31
00:06:26,721 --> 00:06:41,975
A 1991 report by Africa Watch, part of Human Rights Watch, concluded that the government's counter-insurgency tactics, including blocking food and trade in rebel areas, made the famine far deadlier.

32
00:06:44,175 --> 00:07:01,113
By the late 1980s, the Soviet Union was cutting back its support. In 1989, senior officers tried to overthrow Mengistu while he was abroad. The coup failed, and many of its leaders were killed or executed.

33
00:07:02,213 --> 00:07:11,173
Rebel armies from Tigray and Eritrea kept advancing. By May 1991, they were closing in on the capital.

34
00:07:12,273 --> 00:07:24,519
On the twenty-first of May 1991, Mengistu fled to Zimbabwe. A week later, the rebels took Addis Ababa. Eritrea later became an independent country.

35
00:07:26,719 --> 00:07:41,929
The new government set up a special prosecutor to try members of the Derg. The trial of Mengistu and dozens of his officials began in 1994 and lasted twelve years. He was tried in his absence.

36
00:07:43,029 --> 00:07:55,275
In December 2006, the court found him guilty of genocide. Ethiopian law, unlike the international definition, includes the destruction of political groups.

37
00:07:56,375 --> 00:08:05,441
He was sentenced to life in prison. In 2008, on appeal, Ethiopia's Supreme Court changed the sentence to death.

38
00:08:06,541 --> 00:08:16,141
Zimbabwe refused to hand him over. He was protected for years by Robert Mugabe's government, and he stayed in Harare after Mugabe fell.

39
00:08:17,241 --> 00:08:27,055
In interviews, Mengistu has denied genocide. He has said he was defending the revolution and the unity of Ethiopia against its enemies.

40
00:08:29,255 --> 00:08:40,156
In Addis Ababa, a museum dedicated to the victims of the Red Terror opened in 2010. Its walls are covered with the photographs of the dead.

41
00:08:41,256 --> 00:08:53,651
Some Ethiopians still credit his era with land reform, literacy and defending the country's borders. But those achievements cannot be separated from the terror used to hold power.

42
00:08:54,751 --> 00:09:07,551
The bottles smashed in Revolution Square were a promise of blood, and Ethiopians paid it. As of 2025, the man who made that promise had never served a day of his sentence.
